WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.600 --> 00:00:11.900
Okay. Cool. Hey, everyone. How's everyone feeling?

00:00:12.760 --> 00:00:14.760
We got some energy?

00:00:15.340 --> 00:00:20.140
Alright. Awesome. That--that lunch is digested, and you're ready to learn.

00:00:20.140 --> 00:00:22.620
Okay, so I am Quincy,

00:00:22.620 --> 00:00:26.900
and I'm going to talk about how to write technical blog posts

00:00:26.900 --> 00:00:29.340
that people will actually read.

00:00:29.340 --> 00:00:32.480
That--that "actually read" part is important.

00:00:32.660 --> 00:00:36.660
So, you may be wondering: why is this developer talking about writing?

00:00:37.440 --> 00:00:40.720
Well, I worked as a journalist for about three years after college,

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:46.700
and I would say that technical blogging was absolutely critical

00:00:46.700 --> 00:00:49.900
to people actually realizing that Free Code Camp existed

00:00:50.080 --> 00:00:52.340
and to our early community growth.

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.360
We're a small nonprofit.

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:59.100
We don't have, like, a lot of resources for advertising or for public relations

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:03.300
so we turn to trying to write articles worth reading.

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:08.700
So I've been writing very actively on both Medium and on Quora,

00:01:08.700 --> 00:01:13.820
and I also edit Free Code Camp's Medium publication,

00:01:13.960 --> 00:01:19.800
which publishes a whole lot of articles on development, design, and data science.

00:01:21.820 --> 00:01:25.880
So my goal is for all of you to walk out of here

00:01:25.880 --> 00:01:31.240
with both the confidence and the tools that you need to be able to write technical articles

00:01:31.240 --> 00:01:33.960
that people will sit down and actually invest the time in reading.

00:01:34.540 --> 00:01:37.020
So there are really three elements to this.

00:01:37.020 --> 00:01:39.579
First, you need substance.

00:01:39.580 --> 00:01:44.080
Second, you need to package that substance for consumption.

00:01:44.080 --> 00:01:47.140
And third, you need to go out and publicize it.

00:01:47.680 --> 00:01:49.799
So before we get into that, though,

00:01:49.800 --> 00:01:52.740
I want to see why people are blogging.

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:55.000
I saw a lot of hands go up earlier for people who are blogging.

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:57.800
I'm just gonna go up right now.

00:01:57.800 --> 00:02:01.560
Who is blogging because they want to impress future employers?

00:02:01.570 --> 00:02:03.570
Okay. Quite a few.

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:08.300
All right, who wants to apply recently learned knowledge?

00:02:08.300 --> 00:02:13.440
One of the best ways to reinforce your own learning is to turn around and teach it to somebody else,

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.980
And a technical blog post is an excellent way to do that.

00:02:16.880 --> 00:02:22.260
All right, and who just wants to share their insights with other developers and kind of pay it forward?

00:02:23.100 --> 00:02:24.840
Quite a few of you.

00:02:24.840 --> 00:02:25.980
And--

00:02:25.980 --> 00:02:29.840
Maybe nobody will raise their hand because this is a little immodest,

00:02:29.840 --> 00:02:33.599
but who is interested in becoming a thought leader in a specific domain?

00:02:33.600 --> 00:02:35.720
All right, yeah, you want to--

00:02:35.720 --> 00:02:37.000
So you want to get on the conference circuit.

00:02:37.100 --> 00:02:39.960
You want to get on podcasts like Code Newbie.

00:02:39.960 --> 00:02:44.840
And--and you want to raise awareness of your area of expertise. Great?

00:02:44.849 --> 00:02:50.200
It does not matter, like, regardless of your reason, it does not matter.

00:02:50.220 --> 00:02:55.180
Really the goal should be to just get people to read your articles.

00:02:55.180 --> 00:02:59.680
If you've set out to achieve that one single goal, it will help you with all four of those objectives.

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:03.900
Let's start with substance.

00:03:03.900 --> 00:03:06.400
You need to write something that matters.

00:03:06.400 --> 00:03:09.680
And I will tell you that people quickly--

00:03:09.680 --> 00:03:12.600
they have all these kind of rules of thumb about blogging, like,

00:03:12.640 --> 00:03:14.640
Oh, it should be 600 words,

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.660
and, you know, just quickly get it out there,

00:03:17.660 --> 00:03:19.360
and--and blog every single day.

00:03:19.360 --> 00:03:20.700
I don't buy any of that stuff

00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:22.960
I really think that it's all about substance.

00:03:22.960 --> 00:03:26.700
When you have something to say, that's when you open your mouth and you say it.

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:32.389
And you can really hone in and take that substance and turn it into a whole lot of blog posts.

00:03:32.400 --> 00:03:34.400
This is a guy named Peter-Paul Koch.

00:03:34.400 --> 00:03:38.239
He is the author of the popular Quirks Mode blog.

00:03:38.730 --> 00:03:43.700
And Quirks Mode is really awesome because it's focused just on the quirks

00:03:43.760 --> 00:03:47.540
between different browsers and different versions of browsers,

00:03:47.540 --> 00:03:52.320
so he's--he totally geeked out on the difference between how, uh,

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:58.100
JavaScript is processed by Internet Explorer 10 and mobile Safari and Opera.

00:03:58.140 --> 00:04:02.340
And his blog will show you all the different intricacies of those

00:04:02.340 --> 00:04:08.560
for when you're trying to develop cross-browser applications and those considerations.

00:04:08.560 --> 00:04:10.720
So what did he do? He did his homework?

00:04:10.720 --> 00:04:15.460
He learned a whole lot about this one subject, and he went really deep on it,

00:04:15.460 --> 00:04:21.160
then he turned around and figured out ways that he could write about it in novel ways,

00:04:21.160 --> 00:04:22.680
and that's what you should do.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:26.580
A lot of people may lament that, "Oh, everything's been written."

00:04:26.580 --> 00:04:29.920
"It's all been done." "Every flavor of breakfast cereal has been tried."

00:04:30.680 --> 00:04:33.700
Reality is there are always new ways,

00:04:33.800 --> 00:04:42.160
new permutations that you can toggle and switch around to make new original spins on old topics.

00:04:42.880 --> 00:04:46.700
A lot of blog posts are written either from a hello world,

00:04:46.780 --> 00:04:48.720
absolute beginner perspective,

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.360
and then a lot of them are written from a "I've been coding for 10 years"

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:57.140
There aren't enough articles that are kind of in the middle,

00:04:57.140 --> 00:05:01.660
for example, so experience level is one dimension in which you can play around.

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:05.120
Then there are a lot of opportunities

00:05:05.120 --> 00:05:08.440
for taking something that's been written in a different language

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:11.240
and blogging about how to do it in another language

00:05:11.240 --> 00:05:13.720
or introducing humor, right?

00:05:13.720 --> 00:05:16.960
Humor makes your blog post palatable, so use it liberally.

00:05:16.960 --> 00:05:20.360
And you can even write joke posts that are basically parodying something

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:22.280
but teaching at the same time.

00:05:23.000 --> 00:05:26.840
So here's the quick case study. Want everybody to meet Amber.

00:05:27.400 --> 00:05:31.240
She is a Seattle-based marine biologist by training.

00:05:31.240 --> 00:05:34.980
She got her master's degree in marine biology, and she worked at Disney

00:05:34.980 --> 00:05:41.720
surveying the animals and doing science related to the animals that the Disney had and its parks.

00:05:41.820 --> 00:05:46.940
And unfortunately, marine biology is a field that a lot of people want to get into.

00:05:46.940 --> 00:05:47.960
People love animals.

00:05:47.960 --> 00:05:49.460
It's very competitive.

00:05:49.460 --> 00:05:53.940
Around the holiday season she found herself with her contract not renewed.

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:54.900
To cheer herself up--

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:58.900
She was really pumped. She's like, all right. I'm gonna go see the new Star Wars movie

00:05:58.960 --> 00:06:01.440
Did anybody see the new Star Wars movie?

00:06:01.440 --> 00:06:03.170
Rogue One.

00:06:03.170 --> 00:06:04.200
Okay, awesome.

00:06:04.200 --> 00:06:11.200
So she was really excited because, like, the first time it looked like a Star Wars movie where the protagonist was female.

00:06:11.780 --> 00:06:16.660
But when she walked out of the theater she had a weird sense

00:06:16.660 --> 00:06:20.980
that maybe the protagonist wasn't the female character.

00:06:20.980 --> 00:06:23.480
and sure enough she did the math and she counted up all the words,

00:06:23.520 --> 00:06:27.900
and she found that the main character actually didn't--

00:06:27.920 --> 00:06:30.460
didn't speak as many lines as some of the other characters.

00:06:30.460 --> 00:06:37.120
And she also noticed that there were far fewer female characters in that movie than there were male characters.

00:06:37.260 --> 00:06:40.840
And she was like, well, this seemed like it was marketed as a movie,

00:06:40.840 --> 00:06:43.700
you know, to women to try to get more women in the Star Wars

00:06:43.820 --> 00:06:47.640
So she looked at all the other blockbuster movies, like,

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:50.480
the top 10 grossing movies of 2016.

00:06:50.480 --> 00:06:52.200
She built a bunch of data visualizations.

00:06:52.200 --> 00:06:54.460
She wrote scripts to dive through

00:06:54.460 --> 00:06:59.140
and count all the dialogue and assign the gender to the different characters,

00:06:59.140 --> 00:07:03.340
and she built this beautiful series of data visualizations.

00:07:03.340 --> 00:07:07.460
And so she had the substance; she just needed to sit down and write about it.

00:07:07.460 --> 00:07:09.400
And that's what she did.

00:07:09.400 --> 00:07:12.620
I coached her through it, and we wrote a Medium publication,

00:07:12.740 --> 00:07:15.300
and her article was huge.

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:17.460
It got picked up by Mashable.

00:07:17.460 --> 00:07:20.620
It got picked up by IGN, a whole lot of international press.

00:07:20.620 --> 00:07:22.640
And it raised awareness of this issue.

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:27.420
So she was able to use her scientific skills from being a marine biologist,

00:07:27.420 --> 00:07:31.300
kind of port it over to doing data visualization and data science,

00:07:31.300 --> 00:07:34.640
and as a result, a whole lot of employers

00:07:34.640 --> 00:07:40.200
and prospective freelancing contracts came her way from this article.

00:07:40.280 --> 00:07:42.620
So there's just one blog post, right?

00:07:42.620 --> 00:07:46.520
You only need to write one good blog post if it has good substance.

00:07:46.520 --> 00:07:48.000
So it's a lot of hard work.

00:07:48.020 --> 00:07:50.540
Expect to invest a lot of time and energy.

00:07:50.540 --> 00:07:54.360
Good blog posts don't come easily, not good technical blog posts.

00:07:55.120 --> 00:08:00.560
The second lesson I want you to all walk away from is that packaging is important--

00:08:00.560 --> 00:08:03.260
the way you take your substance,

00:08:03.260 --> 00:08:06.840
the way you package it and present it to the reader

00:08:06.920 --> 00:08:09.340
is very important.

00:08:09.760 --> 00:08:13.240
This is the most expensive vodka in the world

00:08:13.940 --> 00:08:17.320
I'm not a vodka connoisseur or anything,

00:08:17.320 --> 00:08:19.740
but when I saw these bottles, I was immediately sucked in.

00:08:19.740 --> 00:08:21.680
I wanted to learn more about it, right?

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:23.280
It's packaging.

00:08:23.280 --> 00:08:25.360
Well, what's the packaging on a blog post?

00:08:25.360 --> 00:08:26.480
You've got your headline.

00:08:26.480 --> 00:08:28.100
You've got your lead image

00:08:28.100 --> 00:08:33.320
You have the formatting of the text and the sub heads and the pull quotes.

00:08:33.320 --> 00:08:35.800
And then you've got the platform that you publish it in.

00:08:35.870 --> 00:08:42.039
All of those things are considerations that you should keep in mind when it comes to packaging your technical blog post.

00:08:43.130 --> 00:08:46.749
I want to talk about headlines specifically because I want to point out that

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:51.400
that is all that about 95 percent of people are going to read.

00:08:52.000 --> 00:08:54.820
They're scrolling through their--their Twitter feed

00:08:54.820 --> 00:08:57.700
while they're sitting on the toilet or while they're holding the handle on a bus,

00:08:57.740 --> 00:09:00.300
and they're like, "Do I want to read this? Do I want to read this?"

00:09:00.300 --> 00:09:01.960
They read your headline,

00:09:01.960 --> 00:09:06.180
and it's up to your headline to entice them to read the rest of your article.

00:09:06.180 --> 00:09:09.100
So has anybody heard the old adage

00:09:09.100 --> 00:09:12.200
by Abe Lincoln about chopping down trees?

00:09:13.280 --> 00:09:15.280
So...

00:09:15.280 --> 00:09:21.180
if I had eight hours to write a technical blog post,

00:09:21.180 --> 00:09:25.060
I'd spend the first six sharpening the headline.

00:09:25.880 --> 00:09:28.800
It's true. The headline really is--

00:09:28.800 --> 00:09:30.740
Empires have been built on the backs of headlines.

00:09:30.740 --> 00:09:32.320
Has anybody heard of BuzzFeed?

00:09:32.320 --> 00:09:34.860
You know, and there's a science to it.

00:09:34.860 --> 00:09:37.100
There's a lot. You can--you can read up on.

00:09:37.180 --> 00:09:39.100
And you can A/B test your headlines.

00:09:39.100 --> 00:09:42.720
You can show them to your friends and just try to figure out if people will click on them.

00:09:42.720 --> 00:09:44.120
It's really that important.

00:09:44.920 --> 00:09:46.320
And then the images.

00:09:46.320 --> 00:09:48.320
Humans are highly visual creatures.

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:51.400
Like, our our visual cortex is one of the biggest parts of our brain.

00:09:51.460 --> 00:09:53.160
So you've got a cater to that.

00:09:53.160 --> 00:09:55.160
You've got to show people images.

00:09:55.480 --> 00:10:00.000
86% of the top blog posts have images in them,

00:10:00.020 --> 00:10:05.040
and on average, they'll have at least one image every 350 words.

00:10:06.820 --> 00:10:10.200
Some of you may be like well, I can't afford to license really good images.

00:10:10.200 --> 00:10:11.340
Well, there's great news.

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:17.300
There are so many generous photographers out there who take their images and turn them to Creative Commons

00:10:17.300 --> 00:10:20.260
or even Creative Commons 0, which doesn't even require attribution.

00:10:20.260 --> 00:10:21.880
You can just splash their photo up on there.

00:10:21.880 --> 00:10:23.740
And this website Pexels.

00:10:24.760 --> 00:10:29.100
You can search around and find images that are public domain

00:10:29.100 --> 00:10:32.020
or Creative Commons license on a number of platforms.

00:10:33.020 --> 00:10:35.600
So in terms of formatting,

00:10:35.600 --> 00:10:42.900
you need to be sure that you don't get the Picardian wall of text, right?

00:10:42.940 --> 00:10:47.120
People will bounce if they see English comp 101,

00:10:47.120 --> 00:10:48.600
eight-sentence paragraphs.

00:10:49.820 --> 00:10:53.400
Remember Reddit is only an alt tab away.

00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:58.260
So be sure that you show them...

00:10:59.020 --> 00:11:01.800
nice short paragraphs, nice short sentences.

00:11:02.660 --> 00:11:06.040
It's not a matter of sounding like dumbing it down.

00:11:06.040 --> 00:11:07.560
A lot of people look at it that way.

00:11:07.560 --> 00:11:12.900
You can express the exact same concepts in much simpler words

00:11:12.900 --> 00:11:16.720
and you can use short paragraphs and you can use white space.

00:11:16.720 --> 00:11:18.800
You can use subheads you can use pull-quotes.

00:11:18.800 --> 00:11:20.800
All those things to break that up.

00:11:20.860 --> 00:11:23.459
The platform you publish it on makes a big difference.

00:11:24.060 --> 00:11:28.260
These are some of the most popular ways that people publish their technical blog posts.

00:11:28.260 --> 00:11:30.500
So I am a big fan of Medium.

00:11:30.540 --> 00:11:31.760
It's ad-free.

00:11:31.760 --> 00:11:34.380
It's--it's user-supported.

00:11:34.380 --> 00:11:38.600
And it allows for easy export of your content.

00:11:38.660 --> 00:11:41.100
And it has a built-in audience that's quite big.

00:11:41.100 --> 00:11:43.600
They get, like, 86 million visits a month.

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:47.699
And then you can also post on blog--on your own private blog.

00:11:47.700 --> 00:11:49.600
You can just use a GitHub Pages blog

00:11:49.600 --> 00:11:51.959
and create--get your own domain and set it up that way, too.

00:11:51.960 --> 00:11:54.240
There are trade-offs associated with all these.

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:56.128
If you happen to have a whole lot of friends on LinkedIn

00:11:56.128 --> 00:11:58.000
or if you happen to have a whole lot of friends on Facebook,

00:11:58.000 --> 00:11:59.580
that might be a good place to publish, too.

00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:03.440
Notice a lot of celebrities have been blogging on--directly on Facebook.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:07.260
So here's another case study. This is Ohans.

00:12:07.260 --> 00:12:11.640
He is a web developer in Lagos, Nigeria,

00:12:11.640 --> 00:12:14.880
and he created this amazing guide to Flexbox.

00:12:15.900 --> 00:12:23.200
The only problem was he just created markdown files and folders and pushed it to GitHub and left.

00:12:23.200 --> 00:12:24.720
He left it at that.

00:12:24.720 --> 00:12:27.280
Well, you can go one step further

00:12:27.320 --> 00:12:30.059
and actually write an article, a proper article,

00:12:30.060 --> 00:12:31.880
and that's what I convinced him to do.

00:12:31.880 --> 00:12:37.360
So if you look at this, he has a nice headline that accurately describes--

00:12:37.360 --> 00:12:39.080
it's not a clickbait headline--

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:41.300
what the article is, and then he's got some images.

00:12:41.320 --> 00:12:44.380
He breaks up the text with tweets,

00:12:44.380 --> 00:12:45.940
and he's got subheadings.

00:12:45.940 --> 00:12:47.500
He's got short paragraphs, short sentences.

00:12:47.500 --> 00:12:48.560
It's easy to read.

00:12:48.560 --> 00:12:49.600
It's--it's a breezy read

00:12:49.600 --> 00:12:52.940
even though it's about 10,000 words, right?

00:12:52.940 --> 00:12:55.260
Like, you're gonna be reading for about an hour,

00:12:55.260 --> 00:12:58.400
and you're literally going to learn all about Flexbox.

00:12:58.420 --> 00:13:04.800
And it may seem like, well, who has time to sit down and read an hour-long article about flexbox?

00:13:04.840 --> 00:13:07.800
People read 500-word blog posts, right?

00:13:07.800 --> 00:13:11.820
Well, actually, this is one of the most popular technical articles ever published.

00:13:11.820 --> 00:13:14.200
It's been viewed more than a 100,000 times,

00:13:14.200 --> 00:13:17.940
and as a result, he got inquiries from book publishers.

00:13:17.940 --> 00:13:20.600
He even had some course platforms reach out to him

00:13:20.600 --> 00:13:23.860
who wanted him to create some workshops on Flexbox,

00:13:23.860 --> 00:13:26.100
So again, he had substance.

00:13:26.100 --> 00:13:27.500
He knew Flexbox very well.

00:13:27.500 --> 00:13:29.720
He packaged it properly, and he profited.

00:13:30.820 --> 00:13:32.940
Lesson number three: Publicize.

00:13:32.940 --> 00:13:36.000
You may have heard the expression in software development:

00:13:36.000 --> 00:13:40.520
The last 10% of the work takes 90% of the time.

00:13:41.560 --> 00:13:46.100
Well, let me tell you: publicizing your work is the last 10%,

00:13:46.120 --> 00:13:48.340
and it's gonna take 90% of your time.

00:13:48.340 --> 00:13:50.500
There is no build it and they'll come.

00:13:50.500 --> 00:13:55.440
You need to go out and tell people that this exists,

00:13:55.580 --> 00:14:00.660
and you need to actively advocate for people to read your own article.

00:14:00.660 --> 00:14:02.370
One place you can put it is Hacker News.

00:14:02.370 --> 00:14:04.780
I like to call it the Hacker News lottery

00:14:04.780 --> 00:14:06.780
because, honestly, there's like--

00:14:06.780 --> 00:14:09.680
I could publish, like, the best article I've ever written on Hacker News,

00:14:09.680 --> 00:14:11.040
and nobody would notice,

00:14:11.040 --> 00:14:14.840
or some random article that I published might show up at the top one day

00:14:14.920 --> 00:14:16.360
because somebody submitted it.

00:14:16.360 --> 00:14:22.020
It's, like, totally random, and I can tell you I see totally random things on Hacker News.

00:14:22.020 --> 00:14:25.100
You see totally random things at the top of Reddit sometimes.

00:14:26.160 --> 00:14:30.760
But if you keep submitting, every--every time you submit,

00:14:30.780 --> 00:14:32.260
that's a lottery ticket, right?

00:14:32.260 --> 00:14:33.880
And you need to pay to win--

00:14:34.240 --> 00:14:35.500
or you need to play to win.

00:14:35.500 --> 00:14:38.600
So you can also target specific Facebook groups.

00:14:38.640 --> 00:14:41.520
There are a lot of really big Facebook groups.

00:14:41.520 --> 00:14:43.200
Some of them are local to your city.

00:14:43.200 --> 00:14:47.260
Maybe your alumni group on Facebook from your university or from your high school.

00:14:47.260 --> 00:14:48.980
Share it with your friends that way.

00:14:48.980 --> 00:14:51.340
Groups are a great way to spread it.

00:14:51.340 --> 00:14:52.940
You can also use LinkedIn.

00:14:52.940 --> 00:14:54.780
(Laughter)

00:14:54.780 --> 00:14:56.320
People do use LinkedIn groups.

00:14:56.320 --> 00:14:58.100
It does send, like, email digests.

00:14:58.100 --> 00:14:59.680
People might see it.

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:04.660
And then I would also say use other people's audiences.

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:06.389
So if you publish on a Medium publication,

00:15:06.389 --> 00:15:10.000
you're immediately, kind of, hijacking the audience of that publication

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:11.840
for a moment to get their attention

00:15:11.840 --> 00:15:14.740
If you publish it in Forbes or TechCrunch

00:15:14.740 --> 00:15:17.580
or some of these other major publications

00:15:17.580 --> 00:15:21.080
that actually submit, kind of, op-ed reader posts--

00:15:21.080 --> 00:15:24.660
And--and you can actually become a writer for Forbes or a writer for TechCrunch

00:15:24.660 --> 00:15:26.500
and publish right on there,

00:15:26.500 --> 00:15:30.740
and that way you're borrowing their credibility as well as their audience.

00:15:31.780 --> 00:15:33.780
So just to summarize...

00:15:34.200 --> 00:15:36.200
three things to remember:

00:15:36.200 --> 00:15:40.100
Create technical blog posts that are substantial,

00:15:40.140 --> 00:15:42.140
package them for consumption,

00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:44.020
and publicize them like crazy.

00:15:44.020 --> 00:15:48.680
If you do that, you'll write blog posts that people will actually sit down and read.

00:15:48.740 --> 00:15:50.160
Thank you very much.

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:52.160
(Applause)

00:15:59.080 --> 00:16:00.100
Great job.

